:::oak-heading {id="title" level="1"}
Adopting different perspectives
:::

:::oak-heading {id="task-a" level="2"}
Task A: Switching perspectives
:::

:::oak-instruction {id="task-a-instruction"}
Write the same scene from two different perspectives. Begin with a short paragraph in the past tense and third person. Then describe Addie's thoughts in the present tense and first person.
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-1" number="1"}
Write your version of the scene. Use sentence variety and description in the third-person paragraph. Focus on Addie's feelings, thoughts and questions in the first-person paragraph.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-1-response" kind="lines" lines="12"}
:::
:::

:::oak-heading {id="task-b" level="2"}
Task B: Writing an internal monologue
:::

:::oak-instruction {id="task-b-instruction"}
Write an internal monologue for Addie. Use present tense, first person, repetition, contractions, ellipsis, rhetorical questions, informal sentence openers and sensory detail.
:::

:::oak-question {id="question-2" number="2"}
Show what Addie thinks and feels at this moment, including how her senses become overwhelming.

:::oak-answer-space {id="question-2-response" kind="lines" lines="14"}
:::
:::
